  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Telecoms field inspection covers four common jobs: mast and tower surveys, fibre install sign-off, broadcast rigging inspection, and structured cabling testing</li><li>Working at height on masts and towers falls under the Work at Height Regulations 2005; lifting equipment in rigging and tower work falls under LOLER 1998</li><li>Most engineers never fill in a dedicated app on site; they complete it later from memory, and the record suffers</li><li>Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, and dormant users are free, so a firm with a mix of field and office staff only pays for who actually works</li><li>The software captures evidence and generates the report; it does not replace the competent inspector or a certified test instrument</li></ul></div><h2>What telecoms inspection software is actually for</h2><p>Telecoms and media field teams generate a lot of records: tower structural and antenna surveys, fibre install handovers, rigging load inspections, cabling test certificates. Each has its own standard and its own audience, from Openreach handover teams to insurers to broadcast venue managers. What they share is that the record has to be accurate, timestamped and complete, and it has to survive scrutiny years later.</p><p>Software does not make any of these inspections valid. The competent inspector does. What software does is make the record easier to complete correctly on site, harder to forget an item, and faster to deliver. A good tool means the engineer does not reconstruct a survey from memory in the van at the bottom of the site track.</p><h2>The four common telecoms field jobs</h2><h3>1. Mast and tower surveys</h3><p>Most telecoms structural work is working at height, governed by the Work at Height Regulations 2005. A survey covers structural condition, corrosion, feeders and cable management, antenna mounts, aviation lighting and safe access. The record must show what was found, with photographs, at the point of observation. See the mast and tower inspection checklist for the full item list.</p><h3>2. Fibre installation sign-off</h3><p>An FTTP install needs handover evidence: routing, boundary boxes, splice and light-level results, and photos of the finished work. Openreach and other network owners expect a clean handover pack. The tool records the result the engineer reads off the OTDR or power meter; it is not the instrument.</p><h3>3. Broadcast and media rigging</h3><p>Rigging in broadcast and live events uses lifting equipment and accessories that fall under LOLER 1998, with thorough examination and load records. The inspection record has to tie each item to its examination and its rated load.</p><h3>4. Structured cabling testing</h3><p>Data cabling is certified with permanent-link or channel tests against a TIA or ISO class, usually on a Fluke or equivalent tester. The record captures the pass or fail result and the certificate reference. The software stores the result; it is not the certification tester.</p><h2>A note on RF exposure zones</h2><p>Active antennas radiate, and sites can have RF exposure zones assessed against ICNIRP guidelines. Where work happens near live antennas, exclusion zones, permits to work and isolation are part of the safe system of work, set by a competent RF safety assessor. Quickler can record that permits and isolation were in place and photograph the signage and barriers, as evidence. It does not assess exposure levels. Check current ICNIRP guidance and your operator's RF safety policy.</p><h2>What to look for in telecoms inspection software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the record during the survey, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of observation:</strong> each finding tied to a timestamped image, not a folder sorted out that evening</li><li><strong>Clean report output:</strong> a handover pack or survey report the client and insurer accept without explanation</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> charging for every seat punishes firms for their office staff and subcontractors. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, unlimited users</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, engineer, site, every observation, for the insurer or dispute that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the evidence and generates the report. It does not make an inspection valid, and it is not a measuring or certification instrument. It records the result the engineer reads off a calibrated OTDR, power meter or Fluke tester. The competent inspector and the calibrated instrument carry the validity. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is telecoms field inspection software?</h3><p>It is any tool that helps a field engineer record an inspection or survey and produce a report: mast and tower structural surveys, fibre install sign-offs, rigging inspections, cabling tests. The options range from paper, to generic audit apps like iAuditor, to conversation-based tools like quickler that run the workflow over WhatsApp so there is no app to install.</p><h3>Does a tower survey have to follow a specific format?</h3><p>Working at height is governed by the Work at Height Regulations 2005, and structural inspection typically follows the client operator's own survey specification and relevant standards. There is no single statutory report format, so the record must capture the structural, access and antenna items the client and insurer expect. Check the current standards and your client's specification before relying on any template.</p><h3>Can I run telecoms inspections over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. The engineer receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the completed report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does the software replace test instruments?</h3><p>No. Quickler records the result the engineer reads off a certified instrument, such as an OTDR, optical power meter or a Fluke cabling tester, and stores it as evidence with a timestamp and photo. It is not itself a measuring or certification instrument, and it does not make an inspection valid. The competent inspector and the calibrated instrument do that.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/mast-tower-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Mast and tower inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/fibre-installation-sign-off-app-uk.html\">Fibre installation sign-off</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/rigging-inspection-report-uk.html\">Rigging inspection reports</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/structured-cabling-test-report-uk.html\">Structured cabling test reports</a></li></ul>",
